## Rendering nothing

To make a component output nothing, return `null`:

```jsx
function Banner({ message }) {
  if (!message) return null;
  return <div className="banner">{message}</div>;
}
```

`null`, `false`, and `undefined` all render nothing. The component still runs and stays in the tree — it just produces no DOM. The same works from a class `render` method.

## A different question: skipping re-renders

"Prevent rendering" sometimes means "stop an unnecessary re-render", which is a performance concern, not an output one. By default a component re-renders when its parent does, even if its props are identical. To skip that:

```jsx
const Row = React.memo(function Row({ item }) {
  return <li>{item.label}</li>;
});
```

`React.memo` shallow-compares props and skips the render when nothing changed. `useMemo` and `useCallback` help by keeping prop references stable so the memo holds.

## Two different tools

Returning `null` controls what renders. `React.memo` controls whether work is repeated. Do not memoise by default — it has its own cost. Profile first, and reach for it only on components that re-render often with unchanged props.

